00:00:17.249 --> 00:00:19.621
or click the link in the description.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:39.886
I’m going to cut right to the chase.

00:00:39.886 --> 00:00:44.520
We’re looking at the inside of a potato
right now.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.681
Yes, seriously.

00:00:46.681 --> 00:00:52.260
When you take a little slide of that bland,
mushy flesh and put it under the microscope

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:56.511
with polarized light, this is what you see.

00:00:56.511 --> 00:01:02.152
I can’t speak for you, but this, is not
what I expected.

00:01:02.152 --> 00:01:08.470
Potatoes are a great food, but they’re not
exactly the most exciting to look at.

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:14.880
I certainly never expected to see something
that could double as the stained glass windows

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.750
in a Sailor Moon fever dream.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:24.861
But that varied array of colors is built out
of something simple: starch granules.

00:01:24.861 --> 00:01:30.270
Those granules act as storage for the plant’s
carbohydrate supply, which is then stashed

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:33.000
in the potato tubers underground.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.802
The starches are meant to provide energy for
the potato plant.

00:01:36.802 --> 00:01:42.439
But of course, plenty of animals (including
us) like to make use of the potato’s sparkly

00:01:42.439 --> 00:01:44.350
starches as well.

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:50.910
And when you look inside other foods, like
passion fruit and bananas, you can see those

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:58.504
starches as well, sometimes packed into cells
or at other times, flowing across the slide.

00:01:58.504 --> 00:02:03.539
Perhaps, if you’ve been journeying with
us through the microcosmos for a while now,

00:02:03.539 --> 00:02:10.165
you may have noticed that these images look
very different from our usual sights.

00:02:10.165 --> 00:02:14.560
Of course, that’s partially because we’re
looking at very different things from our

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:16.160
typical fare.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:21.879
Usually we’re looking into pond water or
whatever other fascinating bit of nature that

00:02:21.879 --> 00:02:25.915
James, our master of microscopes, usually
looks at.

00:02:25.915 --> 00:02:30.964
But right now, our sights are coming to us
directly from the kitchen.

00:02:30.964 --> 00:02:36.099
But also, things are looking a bit different
because today, we’re looking through the

00:02:36.099 --> 00:02:39.569
lens of a different master of microscopes.

00:02:39.569 --> 00:02:45.183
Her name is Chloe Savard, and you can follow
on instagram at tardibabe.

00:02:47.154 --> 00:02:53.230
Chloe got her start in microscopy three years
ago, after seeing some videos on instagram

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:55.099
that piqued her interest.

00:02:55.099 --> 00:03:01.560
She started slowly at first, but it’s since
built into a huge passion, helping her see

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.695
the world through that combination of

00:03:03.695 --> 00:03:09.423
artistry and science that microscopy 
inherently blends together.

00:03:09.423 --> 00:03:15.770
These videos of the crystals inside of dragon
fruits are some of our favorites of hers.

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:22.867
It looks like she accidentally dropped a giant
container of neon confetti onto her microscope.

00:03:22.867 --> 00:03:30.330
But those funky shapes are probably a type
of sugar called myo-inositol, which crystallized

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:33.731
as water slowly evaporated from the slide.

00:03:33.731 --> 00:03:39.989
If you watch more of what Chloe sees through
her microscope, you’ll see spectacular colors

00:03:39.989 --> 00:03:45.737
over and over again as she renders different
crystals in vivid terms.

00:03:45.737 --> 00:03:51.650
And she’s able to do this using a type of
light called polarized light, which restricts

00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:55.660
the electrical field vibrations of the light
to one plane.

00:03:55.660 --> 00:04:01.133
To our eyes, polarized light looks the same
as non-polarized light.

00:04:01.133 --> 00:04:07.879
But under a microscope, we can see the effect
of polarized light hitting certain types of materials—

00:04:07.879 --> 00:04:10.730
in this case, the crystals in our food—

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:13.785
as it forms a dazzling tableau.

00:04:13.785 --> 00:04:19.019
This effect has been part of the excitement
of microscopy for Chloe, especially because

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:24.717
she loves the saturated colors she’s learned
to bring out with her microscope.

00:04:24.717 --> 00:04:30.725
That’s how she’s able to see the stone
cells inside of fruits like pears and blueberries.

00:04:30.725 --> 00:04:34.949
These cells aren’t alive, but they still
serve a purpose:

00:04:34.949 --> 00:04:40.765
to support the fruit, and sometimes even to
protect them from threats like weevils.

00:04:40.765 --> 00:04:46.310
Their walls are full of sturdy lignin, which
is responsible for the gritty texture you

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:49.397
feel when you bite into a pear.

00:04:49.397 --> 00:04:54.925
Even though these aren’t like the microbial
life we usually watch under the microscope,

00:04:54.925 --> 00:05:00.850
there’s a similar feeling to them, like
we’re unlocking this tiny world that’s

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:04.033
responsible for everything we experience.

00:05:04.033 --> 00:05:10.930
It’s just that instead of, say, a tiny algae
responsible for the air we breathe or a parasite

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:17.190
that can eat away at a body, we’re experiencing
an invisible universe responsible for the

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:21.989
way that our foods feel and 
taste and sustain us.

00:05:21.989 --> 00:05:28.330
Like have you ever eaten a pineapple and immediately
scrunched your face up in pain?

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:36.100
Well, that might be due to these structures called 
raphides made from calcium oxalate crystals.

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:43.470
A number of other fruits and plants have raphides,
and there are a few theories about what raphides

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:44.720
actually do.

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:49.920
Given that they look a lot like needles, 
one idea is that they might function

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:56.174
as protection, prickling away at the mouth
of anyone who dares eat the plant.

00:05:56.174 --> 00:06:01.930
But crystals aren’t the only surreal sight
buried in the microscopic worlds of fruits

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:03.480
and vegetables.

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:09.350
If you look at the inside of an onion or a
pumpkin, you can see a streaming motion within

00:06:09.350 --> 00:06:15.306
the cells called cytoplasmic streaming, or
cyclosis, though we have the movement

00:06:15.306 --> 00:06:17.071
sped up a bit here.

00:06:17.071 --> 00:06:24.150
This motion is a cell at work, using a network
of actin and myosin to carry organelles and

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:30.130
other molecules around so they can carry out
various tasks in distant regions.

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:36.011
We’ve been looking at a lot of fruits and
vegetables, but that’s not all Chloe looks at.

00:06:36.011 --> 00:06:42.169
If you go to her Instagram, you’ll see videos
of some of our favorite microcosmos friends,

00:06:42.169 --> 00:06:45.557
like tardigrades and diatoms and worms.

00:06:45.557 --> 00:06:52.620
Like James, she has an office full of stinky
jars that are full of samples taken from ponds.

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:58.289
But there has been one major challenge to
her love of searching for microscopic life

00:06:58.289 --> 00:07:00.040
in ponds:

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:01.285
winter.

00:07:01.285 --> 00:07:07.804
In Montreal, the cold temperatures often keep
her from finding new samples for months.

00:07:07.804 --> 00:07:11.930
That’s why Chloe started looking at fruits
and vegetables.

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:14.536
It was meant to help her bide the time.

00:07:14.536 --> 00:07:19.227
But after she looked at the inside of a potato,
she was hooked.

00:07:19.227 --> 00:07:26.139
She’d never expected starch to be so beautiful,
or to find similar structures in different

00:07:26.139 --> 00:07:28.027
types of molecules.

00:07:28.027 --> 00:07:34.380
But there they were, an array of striking
colors apparent under the microscope.

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:40.923
Compared to pond samples, which can rely on
hours and hours of patience to sort through,

00:07:40.923 --> 00:07:46.180
fruits and vegetables offered something immediate,
which meant she could experiment with the

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:51.979
artistic side of microscopy in a different
way as well, learning to bring out those structures

00:07:51.979 --> 00:07:53.542
and colors.

00:07:53.542 --> 00:08:00.665
There is both an art and a science to turning
the fruit bowl into a subject in this way,

00:08:00.665 --> 00:08:06.752
crafting a still life that reveals itself
to not be so still after all.

00:08:06.752 --> 00:08:12.731
With every beam of light, Chloe takes us on
a tour of something we see every day, and

00:08:12.731 --> 00:08:15.312
helps us see it through her eyes:

00:08:15.312 --> 00:08:22.080
as something surreal and beautiful and mind-blowing
that is just within our reach.

00:08:24.693 --> 00:08:31.043
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

